#include <vector>
#include <unordered_map>
using namespace std;

vector<int> twoSum(vector<int>& a, int t) {
    unordered_map<long long, int> seen;    // value -> index, only indices < i
    for (int i = 0; i < (int)a.size(); ++i) {
        auto it = seen.find((long long)t - a[i]);   // find() never inserts; long long: margin
        if (it != seen.end()) return {it->second, i};
        seen[a[i]] = i;                    // ... and only now join the range
    }
    return {};
}
```
:::
:::

::: {.p-complexity}
**Check every pair** is $O(n^2)$ time, $O(1)$ space. **Index the whole array first** is $O(n)$ expected time and $O(n)$ space, with two passes. **Query the seen map, then join it** is $O(n)$ expected time and $O(n)$ space in one pass, and stops you from ever writing the self-pair bug; on `unordered_map` with `int` keys, remember @sec-hash-anti if the input is adversarial.
:::

::: {.p-edge}
- $t = 2x$ with two copies of $x$: the answer is the pair of copies. Query-first finds it; insert-first would pair the first copy with itself.
- A single element with $a_i = t/2$ is not a solution: the two indices must differ, and query-first never reports $(i, i)$.
- Negatives: the complement $t - x$ can lie outside the range of the values; a hash map does not care, a frequency array would need an offset (@sec-arr-p5).
- Overflow in C++: with $|a_i|, |t| \le 10^9$, both $a_i + a_j$ and $t - a_i$ can reach $\pm 2\cdot10^9$. That still fits in a 32-bit `int` (limit about $2.147\cdot10^9$), but only just; the listings compute in `long long` so that a variant with slightly larger values cannot overflow (@sec-arr-cost).
:::

::: {.practice}
- [CSES 1640 · Sum of Two Values](https://cses.fi/problemset/task/1640) — the same problem with 1-based positions and $n$ up to $2\cdot10^5$; print `IMPOSSIBLE` when no pair exists.
- [CF 1931D · Divisible Pairs](https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/1931/D){rating="1300"} — the complement of $a_i$ is a pair of remainders, $(-a_i \bmod x,\ a_i \bmod y)$, looked up before inserting.
:::


[**From one pair to using each element once.** Two Sum stops at the first pair. Max Number of K-Sum Pairs keeps pairing, and each element may be used once, so the map holds counts and the pairs consume them.]{.flow-bridge}


### Problem: Max Number of K-Sum Pairs {#sec-hash-p1-b}

::: {.p-statement}
Given $a$ ($1 \le n \le 10^5$, $1 \le a_i \le 10^9$) and $k$ ($1 \le k \le 10^9$), find the maximum number of pairs of **distinct indices** with $a_i + a_j = k$. Each index may be used in at most one pair.

Source: [LC 1679 · Max Number of K-Sum Pairs](https://leetcode.com/problems/max-number-of-k-sum-pairs/){.solved difficulty="Medium"}
:::

::: {.p-intuition}
A value $x$ can only ever pair with the single value $k - x$ — copies of a value are interchangeable. So the answer is determined per complementary *value* pair: $\min(\text{cnt}[x], \text{cnt}[k-x])$ when $x \ne k - x$, and $\lfloor \text{cnt}[x] / 2 \rfloor$ when $x = k - x$. One greedy scan over the array realises exactly that.
:::

::: {.p-approach}
1. **Nested-loop greedy.** For each unused $i$, scan for the first unused $j > i$ with $a_i + a_j = k$ and pair them: $O(n^2)$. Greedy is safe here because only complements can pair — taking a possible pair can never make a better one unavailable.
2. **One count-map pass.** Keep `cnt` = counts of *unpaired* values seen so far. At $x$: if `cnt[k - x] > 0`, spend one of them and count a pair; else store $x$ (@sec-hash-complement with counts instead of indices). $O(n)$ expected.
3. (Alternative: sort and move two pointers inward from both ends — $O(n\log n)$, no hash map, nothing to attack. It is the stress test's third opinion below, and @sec-tp develops it.)

**Invariant.** `cnt` holds exactly the values read so far that have not yet been spent on a pair.
:::

The buttons are in order. Each one keeps the previous solution and changes only what that form changes.

::: {.panel-tabset .solution-forms}

#### Pair by rescanning for a complement

::: {.form-next}
For each element, sweep the rest of the array for something that completes it to $k$; when found, mark both used. Faithful greedy, obviously maximal by the interchangeability argument — and $O(n^2)$ at $n = 10^5$, a billion pair tests, far over budget.
:::

::: {.panel-tabset group="lang"}
