#include <vector>
#include <map>
using namespace std;

int equalPairs(vector<vector<int>>& g) {
    int n = (int)g.size(), res = 0;
    map<vector<int>, int> cnt;            // vector<int> is less-comparable,
    for (auto& row : g) ++cnt[row];       // but has no std::hash — use map
    for (int c = 0; c < n; ++c) {
        vector<int> col;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) col.push_back(g[i][c]);
        auto it = cnt.find(col);
        if (it != cnt.end()) res += it->second;
    }
    return res;
}
```
:::
:::

::: {.p-complexity}
**Compare every row against every column** is $O(n^3)$ time, $O(n^2)$ for the copied columns. **Hash the row tuples, query each column** is $O(n^2)$ expected time (each tuple is built and hashed in $O(n)$) and $O(n^2)$ space for the stored row keys. In C++ the row vector is a *tree* key (`map`, $O(n^2 \log n)$ — `std::hash` has no default for `vector<int>`, and writing one here buys little at $n = 200$); Python hashes tuples natively (@sec-hash-keys).
:::

::: {.p-edge}
- $n = 1$: the single cell is both row and column — answer $1$.
- All-equal grid: every pair matches, $n^2$.
- A `list` is not hashable — `tuple(row)` is not decoration, it is the requirement (@sec-hash-sets).
:::

::: {.practice}
- [CF 1974C · Beautiful Triple Pairs](https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/1974/C){rating="1400"} — tuples as keys again, now with one position masked out, and inclusion–exclusion to count pairs that differ in exactly one place.
- [LC 205 · Isomorphic Strings](https://leetcode.com/problems/isomorphic-strings/){difficulty="Easy"} — a canonical form: rewrite each string as the sequence of first-occurrence indices of its letters, and compare the two tuples.
:::


## Pattern 3: Distinct values and nearby duplicates {#sec-hash-p3}

**From counting matches to asking whether one exists.** Pattern 2 counted pairs inside groups. Many questions only ask whether a value was seen at all, or seen recently, and a set, or a map to the last index, answers that.

**Recognise it.** The question is about *existence* rather than counting: "are there two equal values?", "how many distinct?", "the same value within $k$ indices?" A set answers "have I seen this *at all*"; a map to the **latest index** answers "have I seen it *recently*" — one lookup replaces an entire scan of the past (@sec-hash-complement's table with time attached).

::: {.callout-important title="Template"}
```text
seen = set()                         # or: last = {}   value -> newest index
for i, x in enumerate(a):
    if answer(x in seen):            # or: i - last.get(x, -inf) <= k
        return yes
    seen.add(x)                      # or: last[x] = i
return no                            # distinct count: len(seen) at the end
```

**Invariant.** Before position $i$, the structure summarises exactly the values of $[0, i)$ — as a set, or as each value's most recent position (which is all any distance question can use).
:::

**Complexity.** Time $O(n)$ expected, space $O(\text{distinct})$. The sorting alternative is $O(n\log n)$ guaranteed and worth stating in interviews as the no-adversary fallback (@sec-hash-anti).

### Problem: Distinct Numbers {#sec-hash-p3-a}

::: {.p-statement}
Given $n$ numbers ($1 \le n \le 2\cdot10^5$, $1 \le x_i \le 10^9$), print the number of **distinct** values.

Source: [CSES 1621 · Distinct Numbers](https://cses.fi/problemset/task/1621){.solved}
:::

::: {.p-intuition}
"Distinct" is the hash set's reason to exist: insert everything, read the size. The sorting alternative exists too, and is worth knowing because it is the guaranteed version and generalises to "sorted distinct list".
:::

::: {.p-approach}
1. **Sort, then count changes.** Sort and count positions where $b_i \ne b_{i-1}$: $O(n\log n)$ guaranteed, $O(1)$ extra beyond the copy.
2. **Insert into a set.** $\Theta(n)$ expected, one line — `len(set(a))`.

**Invariant.** After processing $[0,i)$, the set holds exactly the distinct values of that range.
:::

The buttons are in order. Each one keeps the previous solution and changes only what that form changes.

::: {.panel-tabset .solution-forms}

#### Sort and count the changes

::: {.form-next}
Duplicates sit next to each other after sorting, so the distinct count is one plus the number of *adjacent changes*. $O(n\log n)$, deterministic, and it also hands you the distinct values themselves if you need them — something the size of a set cannot do.
:::

::: {.panel-tabset group="lang"}
