## Pattern 4: Majority voting {#sec-hash-p4}

**From a table to two variables.** Every pattern so far stored one entry per distinct value. When the question is which value fills more than a fixed fraction of the array, cancelling pairs of different values finds it with $O(1)$ memory and no table at all.

**Recognise it.** The question is "which value appears **more than a fixed fraction** of the time?" — above $n/2$, above $n/3$. The map in your hand would answer it in $O(n)$ space, but a *guaranteed* fraction is leverage: you can settle the question with one or two candidate counters and $O(1)$ memory (@sec-hash-vote). When you see "given that it always exists, find the majority in $O(1)$ space", the answer is Boyer–Moore cancellation, and the interview is really testing whether you can *prove* the survivor is the majority.

::: {.callout-important title="Template"}
```text
cand, count = None, 0                    # threshold n/(k+1): keep k counters
for x in a:
    if count == 0:   cand, count = x, 1  # slot empty: adopt x
    elif x == cand:  count += 1          # agree: reinforce
    else:            count -= 1          # disagree: cancel one pair
return cand                              # n/2 version: guaranteed
# n/3 version: two seats, then count BOTH survivors again - never skip it
```

**Invariant.** After any prefix, exactly `count` copies of `cand` are "standing": every other element of the prefix has been removed in a pair of two *different* values. A value with more than $n/2$ copies cannot be removed completely, because each removal takes at most one of its copies together with one other element, and there are fewer than $n/2$ other elements.
:::

**Complexity.** Time $O(n)$, space $O(1)$ — versus the map's $O(n)$ space. The $n/3$ variant keeps two candidates and needs one extra counting pass, so it is still $O(n)$ time and $O(1)$ space. If a majority is not promised, add the verification pass even for $n/2$.

### Problem: Majority Element {#sec-hash-p4-a}

::: {.p-statement}
Given $a$ of length $1 \le n \le 10^5$, return the majority element — the value occurring more than $\lfloor n/2 \rfloor$ times. **It always exists** in every input, so no "not found" case is needed.

Source: [LC 169 · Majority Element](https://leetcode.com/problems/majority-element/){.solved difficulty="Easy"}
:::

::: {.p-intuition}
`Counter(a).most_common(1)` is the first thought and a fine implementation — but the follow-up is always the same: *"now do it in $O(1)$ memory."* The guarantee is the whole point: a value with more than half the copies **cannot be cancelled** if you repeatedly delete pairs of *distinct* elements — every deleted pair burns at most one of its copies while removing one non-majority, so majority copies always survive to the end (@sec-hash-vote). Boyer–Moore performs exactly those deletions without touching the array, tracking only "who is ahead, and by how much".
:::

::: {.p-approach}
1. **Count everything.** Histogram with `Counter` (or a frequency array when the value range is small); return the argmax. $O(n)$.
2. **Sort and take the middle.** After sorting, the majority owns more than half the positions, so it must sit at index $n/2$. $O(n\log n)$ time, $O(1)$ extra space.
3. **Cancel pairs: Boyer–Moore.** One pass with `(cand, count)`: adopt when the counter is at zero, reinforce on agreement, burn one on disagreement. $O(n)$ time, $O(1)$ space; the invariant above is the proof.

**Invariant.** After any prefix, exactly `count` copies of `cand` are "standing", and every other element of the prefix has been removed in a pair of two different values. The majority value cannot be removed completely, because each removal takes at most one of its copies together with one of the fewer than $n/2$ other elements — so it is the candidate at the end.
:::

The buttons are in order. Each one keeps the previous solution and changes only what that form changes.

::: {.panel-tabset .solution-forms}

#### Count everything, take the top

::: {.form-next}
The histogram reading of "more than $\lfloor n/2 \rfloor$": count each value once, take the largest count. Straight, $O(n)$ expected, $O(n)$ memory — the memory is the only thing to fix.
:::

::: {.panel-tabset group="lang"}
